course: dictionary
lesson: 14933
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. The word regiment can mean a group of soldiers, a very large number, or the act of controlling people strictly. «The regiment marched through the town at dawn.» Soldiers, marching and dawn show it is a group of soldiers.
explain: Informal meanings are looser and more playful. «There was a regiment of ants marching across the kitchen floor.» Ants are not soldiers, so regiment here must mean a very large number. Always ask: does my meaning fit the sentence?
explain: As a verb, regiment means to control people very strictly so that everything is in order. «The coach wanted to regiment every minute of the training.» The clues are strict and every minute, so the coach is ordering everything very carefully.
fact: A regiment is a large group of soldiers in an army, led by a colonel.
fact: Informally, a regiment can mean a very large number of people or things.
fact: To regiment means to control people very strictly, so that everything is ordered.
fact: «A regimented routine» is a day with strict rules and set times for everything.
fact: In history, regiments had their own names, flags and uniforms.
exercise: «The regiment marched across the field in neat lines.» What is a regiment in this sentence? | A kind of flag | A group of friends on a school trip | A small pet | *A large group of soldiers led by a colonel
exercise: «Mum said there was a regiment of socks under my bed.» What does regiment mean here? | *A very large number | A soldier with a gun | A quiet room | Strict rules
exercise: «Their day was so regimented that they never had time to play.» What does regimented suggest? | Lots of fun and games | Wet and grey | *Strict rules and everything in order | Quiet and cosy
test: «The sergeant wanted to regiment the new recruits.» What does regiment mean as a verb here? | To lose their way | To cook slowly | *To control people very strictly | To sing loudly
test: «A regiment of birds flew over the school.» What does regiment mean? | A bird's wing | *A very large number | A small nest | A kind of song
exam: «The schedule regimented every minute of the school day.» Which meaning fits best? | To lose track of time | To paint the classroom walls | *To control everything strictly so it is in order | To let everyone do as they like
